Ofwat has a temporary opportunity for a Contractor on sub-lot 3A of the Pan-Regulators' Framework for Economic, Financial & Related Consultancy Services to provide three interim policy support roles for the PR19 programme, as a packaged offer (i.e. bidding for all three roles as part of one packaged bid.)

Key responsibility and outputs
The initial assessment of plans (IAP) starts when companies submit their business plans on 3 September 2018. We will publish the results of our IAP on 31 January 2019. During the IAP Ofwat will assess the quality, ambition and innovation of these plans across 9 test areas and based on this assessment, will categorise plans into one of four categories: exceptional; fast track; slow track; and significant scrutiny. The 9 test areas we will assess are:
1.	Engaging customers:
2.	Addressing affordability and vulnerability;
3.	Delivering outcomes for customers;
4.	Securing long term resilience;
5.	Targeted controls, markets and innovation;
6.	Securing cost efficiency;
7.	Aligning risk and return;
8.	Accounting for past delivery; and
9.	Securing confidence and assurance. 
You will be part of the team responsible for testing the quality, ambition and innovation of company business plans in test areas 1, 2 and 3 from the list above. More information on what we will be assessing in these test areas can be found in Appendix to chapter 14 of our final PR19 methodology.

Additional information: For further information, please contact: procurement@ofwat.gsi.gov.uk

Islington Council invites suitable expressions of interest from suppliers for Mental Health Awareness Training in Camden and Islington.

This is a joint commission by Islington Council And Camden Clinical Commissioning Group for similar training services in both boroughs. The commissioning is led by Islington Council.


Current status / Background 

Promoting positive mental health and well-being is a strategic priority for both Camden and Islington. In recent years stakeholders and commissioners have worked to increase the capacity and capability of communities and key front-facing services to help themselves and others when experiencing mental health distress and to tackle stigma and discrimination around mental health. Working to raise awareness and decrease stigma encourages more people to help themselves and others to access support for mental health problems at an early stage.

The mental health awareness training service will sit within a comprehensive approach to mental health promotion and suicide prevention in both boroughs. The overall aims of the mental health promotion programme are to:

â¢	raise awareness and understanding of mental health and services amongst key local workforce in order to recognise and support mental health distress and problems
â¢	raise awareness and develop a better understanding of mental health among Camden and Islingtonâs population, particularly for at-risk groups;
â¢	encourage help seeking behaviour or self-help;
â¢	decrease local inequalities (particularly in timely access to services); 
â¢	raise awareness of local mental health services;
â¢	reduce stigma and discrimination around mental health at community level and in the work place;
â¢	promote recovery and positive outcomes for young people and adults with mental health problems;
â¢	reduce the suicide rates in Camden and Islington through better support and early recognition of mental distress and problems, including self-care, familiarity with local resources and early access to treatment.

The requirement 

The service will deliver high quality, evidence-based mental health awareness training courses to adults in the London Boroughs of Camden and Islington. The majority of these will be Mental Health First Aid (MHFA) and Youth Mental Health First Aid (YMHFA) courses, but the service will also include development and delivery of 1-day mental health awareness trainings and may include suicide prevention training and MHFA for Schools and Colleges. Training will be delivered both to local public-facing workforces and to local resident communities. 

The training will improve traineesâ skills, knowledge and confidence in discussing mental health issues, reduce the stigma associated with mental ill-health and increase knowledge of local services.

Applicants must have a proven track record of delivering adult learning and training courses and demonstrate an in-depth understanding of the mental health needs of Camden and Islingtonâs popula

Highways England is in the early stages of developing a scalable free-flow charging solution for commencement of live service between 2021 and 2024, which will include schemes such as Dartford-Thurrock River Crossing. The solution would be a potential enabler to realising any longer term aspiration of interoperability between charging schemes and a smarter integrated service for the customer. Highways England wishes to speak to the market to gain their insights and experience in this field which will be fed into the development and make-up of the scalable free-flow charging solution. 

We anticipate the following industries will be interested in this opportunity:
- Tolling and free-flow charging providers;
- CCTV and ANPR camera manufacturers;
- Intelligent Transport Systems (ITS) providers;
- Field engineers and maintainers;
- Contact centre and Business Process Outsourcing (BPO) companies;
- Civil enforcement and debt management providers;
- Payment solution providers (e.g. merchant acquirer services); and
- Software vendors.

Interested parties are requested to read the pre-procurement briefing and to confirm their attendance at the market engagement day by emailing TechnologyProcurement@highwaysengland.co.uk by 5pm on 5th February 2018 should they wish to attend. Attendance is strictly restricted to 1 attendee per organisation. Lunch is provided. Interested parties should also outline any specific dietary or accessibility requirements.

The VOA holds a large number of historic records on microfilm. The organisation is legally obliged to retain these records for long periods of time, but frequently has need of the stored information when doing business as usual casework. 

The business has hitherto made use of an all-in-one microfilm viewer and printer to retrieve stored information, but this machine is no longer fit for purpose. The VOA requires a modern, digital solution.

Additional information: The VOA requires a machine that can electronically scan microfilms. The machine must be: 

- compatible with 3M Cartridge Microfilm System cassettes, with a tape width of 16mm;
- compatible with Windows 7 and Windows 10 PC systems;
- capable of outputting the scanned images as PDF files;
- supplied with a comprehensive instruction manual that can facilitate in-house installation, set-up, and use;
- powered by either mains electricity or the PC to which it is connected;
- light enough in weight to be lifted and carried by a single individual. 

The VOA requires delivery of the machine no later than 05/03/2018.

The Council / SEGfL are seeking a service provider for a software framework that will offer a range of software solutions to the users of the framework via an online portal.

The intended commencement date for the framework is 2nd January 2025, for a period of 3+1 years.

The framework will be promoted by SEGfL as a founding member of the National Educational Network (NEN) to over 32,000 educational establishments in the UK.

The list of titles is not yet fully determined and will expand over the term of this arrangement. It will be drawn from all categories of software used in educational establishments, local authorities, and VCSE / 3rd sector organisations. This will include, but not be limited to:

â¢ Microsoft and other application licensing;
â¢ Firewall, Anti-virus and other security software;
â¢ Commercial and educational filtering products and licenses;
â¢ Educational software; and
â¢ Other commercial off-the-shelf (COTS) software;

Related services for software purchased under the framework, or software already deployed by a user of the framework will also be made available under this software framework including but not limited to the following:
â¢ Support and maintenance;
â¢ Renewals of existing software subscriptions;
â¢ Software asset management;

Hardware and appliances that are required to fully utilise the software will also be made available under this software framework, but only insofar that they are integral to the solution being sought.

The main objectives of the framework are:
â¢ For users of the software framework to benefit from preferential discounting and pricing that they would otherwise be unlikely able to achieve individually, via the aggregated spend on software by users of the framework;

â¢ To provide users with a purchasing website (portal) presenting an appropriate software catalogue based on framework user type, co-branded as a SEGfL service;
â¢ The portal to deliver a fast, simple and compliant route for users to direct award orders through;
â¢ For the successful supplier to support management of the software framework by suggesting software titles / solutions, negotiating prices and terms with software vendors, marketing the framework to users, managing licences, recording transactions and reporting regularly to the Contracting Authority / SEGfL.

It is intended that the framework can be utilised by the following entities:

Educational establishments listed here:

https://get-information-schools.service.gov.uk/Search?SelectedTab=Establishments and updated from time to time

Local Authorities including County Councils, City Councils, District Councils, Borough Councils, Unitary Authorities, Metropolitan Districts and London Boroughs listed here:

